// BCD code - Hello World! :: RAiNBOW_Hello-World
//   colour     : not assigned by Linguist
//   layer      : full (byte-balanced rainbow bar)
//   upstream   : https://github.com/quxianp/RAiNBOW_Hello-World
//   reference  : see https://esolangs.org/ and https://rosettacode.org/

# Packed BCD stores a decimal digit in four bits, with 1111 used as a
# sign nibble -- so digits run 0001..1000 for 1..8 and 1010 for zero.
# "Hello World!" has no digits in it, so the packed-BCD form of the
# string is its characters: each pair packed into one byte.
#
#   'H' = 0x48, 'e' = 0x65 -> nibbles 4 8 6 5
#
# This is why BCD was never adopted for text: it doubles the density for
# digits and costs a nibble for every letter.
